==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmɛnɪs/|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
First attested ''ante'' 1300: from the {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|manace|lang=fro}}, {{term|menace|lang=fro}}, from the {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|minācia}}, from {{term|minax||threatening|lang=la}}, from {{term|minor||I threaten|lang=la}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|menaces}}

# a [[perceived]] [[threat]] or [[danger]]
#* Dryden
#*: the dark '''menace''' of the distant war
# the act of [[threatening]]
# an [[annoying]] and [[bothersome]] person

====References====
* “<span class="plainlinks">[http://ed2.oed.com/cgi/display/00143950 menace, ''n.'']</span>” listed in the ''[[w:Oxford English Dictionary|Oxford English Dictionary]]'' [2nd Ed.; 1989]

===Etymology 2===
First attested in 1303: from the {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|menacer|lang=fro}}, {{term|manecier|lang=fro}}, {{term|manechier|lang=fro}} and the {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|manasser|lang=fro}}, from the assumed {{etyl|VL.|en}} *{{term||mināciāre}}, from the {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|minācia}}, whence [[menace#Etymology 1|the noun]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb|menaces|menacing|menaced}} (''transitive'' or ''intransitive'')

# To make threats against (someone); to [[intimidate]].
#: ''to '''menace''' a country with war''
#* Shakespeare
#*: My master {{...}} did '''menace''' me with death.
# To threaten (an evil to be inflicted).
#* Shakespeare
#*: By oath he '''menaced''' / Revenge upon the cardinal.
# To [[endanger]] (someone or something); to [[imperil]] or [[jeopardize]].

====References====
* “<span class="plainlinks">[http://ed2.oed.com/cgi/display/00143951 menace, ''v.'']</span>” listed in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' [2nd Ed.; 1989]

----

